{"meta":{"chapter_title":"Instructor Toolkit for CAT 531","title":"Evidence Collection Checklist","description":"CAT 531 textbook section connecting evidence collection checklist to an auditable evidence-collection checklist row with claim, source, baseline, dimensioned result, disconfirmation, and verification path, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","course":"cat531-supplement","chapter":"08","section":"07","learning_objectives":[{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-01","statement":"Write an auditable evidence-collection checklist row that records the claim, source, baseline, dimensioned result, disconfirmation check, and a verification path a reviewer could follow"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-02","statement":"Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-03","statement":"Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions"},{"id":"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-04","statement":"Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible"}],"concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"prereq_section_ids":["cat531-supplement/08/06"],"practice_ids":["cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_calibration","cat531-supplement_08_07_lo_03_direct_check"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":7,"top_tier_hardened":false,"lo_practice_map":{"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-01":["cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_artifact_signal","cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_annotation_signal","cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_ai_boundary","cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_calibration"],"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-02":["cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_artifact_signal"],"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-03":["cat531-supplement_08_07_lo_03_direct_check"],"cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-04":["cat531-supplement_08_07_lo_03_direct_check"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Evidence Collection Checklist\n\n<figure-block caption=\"Organizational reference model connecting Evidence Collection Checklist, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning to an evidence-based revision decision.\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat531-supplement/08-07-evidence-collection-checklist.png\" alt=\"Three connected concept cards for Evidence Collection Checklist: Evidence Collection Checklist, Evidence Based Revision, and AI Supported Learning, followed by an evidence-to-revision transfer prompt.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nTeachers make claims about technology all the time: \"the AI tool helped,\" \"engagement went up,\" \"it saved time.\" Most of these claims are hopes wearing the clothes of evidence. The skill in this section is the discipline of evidence collection: writing claims that another person could check, with a named source, a baseline to compare against, a result, and a statement of what would have changed your mind. An evidence-collection checklist is the instrument that turns a teacher's impression into something a colleague or administrator can audit.\n\nThis matters because technology decisions get challenged, and an unverifiable claim collapses under the first hard question. A claim built to be checked survives the question and, more importantly, tells you the truth: sometimes the tool helped only some students, or helped on one dimension and not another. Evidence that can disconfirm your hope is the only kind worth collecting.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Write an auditable evidence-collection checklist row that records the claim, source, baseline, dimensioned result, disconfirmation check, and a verification path a reviewer could follow.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use annotations, support requests, and revision notes to make a classroom technology decision more defensible.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA teacher writes in a checklist: \"The AI tool improved student writing.\" A department chair asks three questions: improved compared to what, on which dimension, and how would you know if it had not? The teacher has no baseline, no specified outcome, and no comparison. The claim, which felt true, turns out to be untestable. The question this section answers is how to rewrite that single row so it names the artifact, the baseline, the measured result, and the way anyone could re-check it.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nAn evidence row is auditable when a stranger could locate the artifacts, reconstruct the comparison, and re-run the check. Three properties separate a real evidence row from an opinion dressed as one:\n\n- **A named source.** State which artifact, which class, and which date range, so the evidence can be found. \"Improved\" with no referent cites nothing.\n- **A baseline and comparison.** A claim of improvement implies a before and an after measured the same way. Without a baseline, scored on a common instrument, there is no improvement to speak of, only a feeling.\n- **A disconfirmation condition.** Strong evidence states what would have changed your mind and reports whether you looked. Checking whether weaker writers gained less, and finding that they did, bounds the claim honestly instead of overselling it.\n\nA useful structure for an auditable row is: claim, source, baseline, result, disconfirming evidence sought, and how it could be verified by someone else. Reporting separate outcomes matters too; a tool can raise organization scores while leaving argument quality flat, and collapsing those into \"improved writing\" hides the real finding. [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017) is a useful caution here: a dashboard number is not automatically evidence, and a metric is only as good as the comparison and the population behind it. Teacher professional judgment supplies the honesty to bound a claim rather than to inflate it.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nCompare a checklist row that is really an opinion with one built to be audited.\n\n### Annotated judgment call: turning a vague claim into a checked evidence row\n\nScenario: a teacher is filling the evidence-collection checklist that backs a technology decision. A weak row asserts a result; a strong row names the artifact, the source, the date, and how the claim could be verified by someone else.\n\nBefore (a checklist row that is really just an opinion):\n\n> Evidence row: The AI tool improved student writing.\n\nWalk the row against what makes evidence auditable:\n\n1. Source check. \"Improved\" cites nothing. Name the artifact and where it lives (which assignment, which class, which date range).\n2. Comparison check. Improvement implies a before and an after. The before row has no baseline, so the claim cannot be tested.\n3. Disconfirmation check. Strong evidence says what would have changed your mind. The before row admits no possibility of being wrong.\n\nAfter (annotated evidence row):\n\n> Evidence row (08.07): Claim = AI revision coach helped on organization, not on argument quality. Source = 22 paired drafts, period 3 English, Oct 6 to Oct 17, stored in the unit folder. Baseline = pre-AI essays scored on the same 4-point organization rubric. Result = organization mean rose 2.4 to 3.1; argument-quality mean flat at 2.6. Disconfirming evidence I looked for = whether weaker writers gained less; they did, so the claim is bounded to mid-range writers. Verifiable by = a colleague re-scoring 5 random pairs blind.\n\nThe after row is stronger because another teacher can locate the artifacts, see the baseline and the comparison, and re-check the result, which is the difference between collected evidence and a hopeful claim.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"From means to a bounded gain (4-point rubric)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"baseline\",\"label\":\"Baseline mean (pre-AI)\",\"min\":1,\"max\":4,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":2.4},{\"name\":\"post\",\"label\":\"Post-AI mean\",\"min\":1,\"max\":4,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":3.1},{\"name\":\"sd\",\"label\":\"Pooled standard deviation\",\"min\":0.3,\"max\":1.5,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"pts\",\"default\":0.8}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Raw gain (post minus baseline)\",\"unit\":\"pts\",\"expr\":\"post - baseline\"},{\"label\":\"Standardized gain (gain / SD)\",\"unit\":\"SD\",\"expr\":\"(post - baseline) / sd\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"post\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nNotice what the explorer makes visible: a raw gain like 3.1 minus 2.4 equals 0.7 points looks identical whether the standard deviation is small or large, but the standardized gain (the same 0.7 divided by the spread) shrinks as the spread widens. A claim of improvement is only meaningful against a baseline measured on the same instrument and read alongside the spread of scores, which is exactly why an evidence row needs the baseline, not just the after number.\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Using the explorer, explain why reporting only the post-AI mean (say 3.1) without the baseline and the spread can mislead a department chair about whether the tool helped.\" answer=\"The post mean alone has no referent. Without the baseline, there is no before-and-after comparison, so 3.1 could represent a large gain, no change, or even a decline from a higher starting point. And without the spread, even a real raw gain can be tiny relative to how much scores already vary, so it may not be a dependable improvement. Reporting the baseline and the standard deviation lets the chair judge the size and reliability of the gain rather than trusting a single hopeful number.\"></self-explain>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nWhen building an evidence row, name the source first, establish the baseline and comparison second, and state the disconfirmation condition third. Before you treat a row as finished, confirm it answers:\n\n1. **Locate check:** Could a stranger find the exact artifacts from your description?\n2. **Compare check:** Is there a baseline measured on the same instrument as the result?\n3. **Disconfirm check:** What would have changed your mind, did you look, and how could someone re-verify the result?\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\n<inline-check question=\"A row reports: 'Writing improved: post-AI mean was 3.1 on the rubric.' What single addition would most increase its auditability?\" explanation=\"A baseline measured on the same instrument. Improvement is a before-and-after claim; without the pre-AI mean on the same rubric there is nothing to compare 3.1 against, so the claim cannot be tested.\" options='[{\"text\":\"A more confident restatement of the claim.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A baseline (pre-AI mean) scored on the same rubric.\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"A second post-AI mean from a different rubric.\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"A longer description of the assignment.\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nThe most common error is mistaking a number for evidence. A platform dashboard reporting \"85 percent engagement\" is a metric, not a finding, until you know the baseline and the population it describes. A second error is reporting only the outcome that confirms the hope and folding away the dimension that stayed flat; honest evidence reports the flat result too. A third is omitting the disconfirmation condition, which is what separates evidence from advocacy: a claim that could never be wrong is not evidence.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nSuppose you piloted an AI feedback tool in two sections and want to record whether it helped. Name the artifacts: which sections, which assignment, which dates, where the drafts live. Establish the baseline by scoring pre-tool work on the same rubric you will use after. Report the result by dimension, not as a single verdict, so organization and argument are separated. Then state what you checked that could have undercut the claim, such as whether the gains concentrated in students who were already strong, and report what you found. Close with how a colleague could re-score a sample blind to confirm it.\n\nThe artifact is the completed row: source, baseline, dimensioned result, disconfirmation sought, and a verification path another teacher could run.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nRead an evidence row by trying to attack it. Ask where the artifacts are, what the comparison is, and what would have falsified the claim. If the row answers all three, it is auditable; if it answers none, it is an opinion. Keep a two-column note: the claim on the left, the source-baseline-disconfirmation that backs it on the right. A strong row lets a skeptic re-check the result without asking you for anything you did not already write down.\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nAn evidence row meets the standard when a mentor teacher could locate the artifacts, see the baseline, read a dimensioned result, and find a disconfirmation condition. Neat formatting is the last layer; the source, the comparison, and the disconfirmation are load-bearing. If the baseline is missing, annotate the gap and ask BigAL to compare your row against an exemplar that includes a baseline rather than to restate the claim more confidently. A useful closing line reads: \"I named the source and baseline and reported the result by dimension, and I rejected the single sentence 'it improved writing' because it had no baseline and admitted no way to be wrong.\"\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nMove the checklist from an academic outcome to a behavioral one, such as whether an AI nudging tool reduced late assignment submissions. The structure transfers: name the source (which class, which weeks), set a baseline (late-rate before the tool), report the result, and seek disconfirmation (did the drop hold once a holiday week was excluded?). Rebuilding the row for the behavioral claim, rather than reusing the writing example, shows you can apply evidence discipline to any outcome a tool is supposed to affect.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the evidence-collection checklist row packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat531-supplement/08-07-evidence-collection-checklist-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"evidence-collection checklist row (claim, source, baseline, dimensioned result, disconfirmation, verification path)\" course=\"CAT 531\" section=\"08.07\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the evidence-collection checklist row.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the evidence-collection checklist row for CAT 531 08.07. A peer comment points to a transfer move issue connected to critical edtech studies. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"What best shows transfer rather than completion?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\", \"The learner explains how the same decision rule changes under a new audience or constraint.\"]' correct-index=\"3\" conceptid=\"cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531\", \"cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, make sure each row names a locatable source, sets a baseline measured on the same instrument, reports the result by dimension, and states what would have changed your mind and how someone could re-verify it.\n\n## References\n\n- [TPACK](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Technological_pedagogical_content_knowledge). https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1467-9620.2006.00684.x\n- critical edtech studies. https://doi.org/10.4324/9781315886350\n- [AI and education datafication](https://doi.org/10.1080/17439884.2020.1686017)\n- teacher professional judgment. https://doi.org/10.1177/002205741319300304\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist","stem":"An evidence-collection row reads: \"The AI tool improved student writing.\" What is missing for this to count as evidence?","options":["A longer sentence describing the tool.","The artifact, where it is stored, and the date range, so another teacher could locate and inspect it.","A statement that the teacher liked the tool.","The vendor's marketing claims."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"A claim with no cited artifact or source is an opinion. Evidence names what can be located and inspected.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_opinion_as_evidence","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_instructor_toolkit_for_cat_531","cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-01","cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-02"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"A row claims the AI revision coach raised organization scores. What must the checklist include for that claim to be testable?","options":["A note that students seemed happier.","A pre-tool baseline scored on the same rubric, so the before and after can be compared.","The number of students in the class.","The brand name of the tool."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Improvement implies a before and an after measured the same way. Without a baseline the claim cannot be tested.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_no_baseline","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_ai_supported_learning","stem":"A teacher only records the cases where the AI coach seemed to help. What habit makes the evidence stronger?","options":["Collecting even more examples that confirm the tool helped.","Stating what would have changed your mind and checking for it, for example whether weaker writers gained less.","Deleting any result that does not fit the conclusion.","Asking the tool whether it helped."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Strong evidence can be disconfirmed. Looking for the counter-case (who gained less) bounds and strengthens the claim.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_no_disconfirmation","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist","ai_supported_learning"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_collection_checklist_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat531_supplement_evidence_based_revision","stem":"Which checklist entry best makes a result verifiable by someone other than the teacher who collected it?","options":["The result felt clear to me.","22 paired drafts are stored in the unit folder; a colleague can re-score 5 random pairs blind on the same 4-point rubric.","The class agreed the tool was useful.","I remember the scores going up."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Stored artifacts plus a stated re-check method let an independent reader confirm the result, which is what makes it evidence.","misconception_id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_not_verifiable","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["cat531_supplement_evidence_collection_checklist","evidence_based_revision"],"learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-01"]},{"id":"cat531-supplement_08_07_lo_03_direct_check","type":"conceptual","stem":"Provide a concise response that demonstrates this learning objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","expected_answer":"The response accurately demonstrates the section objective: Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions, using relevant section concepts, evidence, or calculations.","explanation":"This direct-evidence item is used when no closed-response item adequately samples the objective. Score it with the section concepts and worked examples as the rubric.","difficulty":"medium","learning_objective_ids":["cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-03","cat531-supplement-08-07-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_opinion_as_evidence","pattern":"logging_an_opinion_as_evidence","description":"Writing a checklist row that asserts a result (the tool improved writing) without naming the artifact, source, or location that would let anyone verify it","trigger":"row states a conclusion with no cited source or artifact","feedback":"Name the artifact, where it lives, and the date range. A claim with no source is an opinion, not evidence.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_no_baseline","pattern":"claiming_improvement_with_no_baseline","description":"Asserting improvement without a before measure, so there is nothing to compare the result against","trigger":"row claims a gain but records no pre-tool baseline","feedback":"Record the baseline. Improvement implies a before and an after measured the same way.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_no_disconfirmation","pattern":"collecting_only_confirming_evidence","description":"Gathering only evidence that supports the hoped-for conclusion and never looking for what would disconfirm it","trigger":"row admits no possibility of being wrong and seeks no counter-case","feedback":"State what would have changed your mind and whether you looked for it. Strong evidence can be disconfirmed.","rail_action":"ask"},{"id":"cat531_supplement_8_7_evidence_not_verifiable","pattern":"result_no_one_else_can_check","description":"Recording a result with no way for a colleague to re-check it, such as no stored artifacts or scoring method","trigger":"row gives no path for an independent re-check","feedback":"Make it verifiable: store the artifacts and name how a colleague could re-score or re-check the result.","rail_action":"practice"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"214cef14e82570e7988ee18bec40ef3a2f9a4631558c7a25dd698fec7540cd6f","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}